WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.600
You point your coding agent at one button.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.540
But most changes have unforeseen consequences.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.740
What if you took every page and component of your UI,

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.720
and laid them out next to each other?

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.740
Now, let’s copy those components

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.100
into every state the user will see:

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.980
error states, empty states, easy ones, hard ones.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.920
StorybookJS has made that easy for the past 10 years.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.700
With coding agents, it’s more useful than ever:

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.880
you can point at the specific page you want changed.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.520
But how can you understand the

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.800
full ripples of a code change?

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.260
Enter Chromatic.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.500
This is a cloud service which renders every

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.820
component of a Storybook to an image.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.040
Change the code, render every component

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.140
to another image, and compare.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.700
This works great, but it requires

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.480
waiting on a cloud service.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.380
At 0.8 cents per image, this was a reasonable

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.620
expense at the speed of human code.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.560
But for a modest library of 1,000 stories,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.700
that’s 8 bucks for a full run,

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.360
each time you even look at a code change!

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.940
Introducing Selfie.dev: a snapshot testing

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.660
tool which runs locally and in the cloud.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.640
Your agent takes its own picture.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.700
No waiting on a cloud rendering service,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.860
and no paying for each image.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.240
541 stories in about 70 seconds.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.740
It works just like git.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.680
Images are stored locally wherever the

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.040
agent is running, and also in the cloud,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.760
so that every commit on GitHub shows the complete

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.180
visual consequences of the code change.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.160
Approvals follow your history too,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.400
so they survive a commit, a rebase, or a merge.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.300
Every image is rendered in the same

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.700
pinned environment, wherever it runs.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.660
Each image is named by a hash of its pixels.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.540
Most changes touch only a few,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.680
so only those few get sent.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.660
If you’re using StorybookJS,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.000
you can start using Selfie locally to review

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.240
your changes right now, at selfie.dev.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.880
If you want to use it in the cloud on your

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.320
GitHub repositories, join our waitlist,

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.220
and get a discount when we launch in mid-November.
